                "content": "# Solving the Cyclic Tangent-Double System\n\n## Key Substitution\n\nThe expression $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ is the **double-angle formula** for tangent: if $t=\\tan\\theta$, then\n$$\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1-\\tan^2\\theta}=\\tan(2\\theta).$$\n\nSetting $x=\\tan\\theta$, the system forces:\n$$y=\\tan(2\\theta),\\quad z=\\tan(4\\theta),\\quad w=\\tan(8\\theta),\\quad x=\\tan(16\\theta).$$\n\nThe **closing condition** $x=\\tan(16\\theta)=\\tan(\\theta)$ gives:\n\n$$16\\theta \\equiv \\theta \\pmod{\\pi} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad 15\\theta = k\\pi \\quad \\text{for some } k\\in\\mathbb{Z}.$$\n\n## Handling the Singular Values $\\pm i$\n\nThe tangent map $\\tan:\\mathbb{C}\\to\\mathbb{C}$ has range $\\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{i,-i\\}$, so I must check these separately.\n\n**If $x=i$:** then $y=\\frac{2i}{1-(-1)}=i$, and by induction $z=w=i$, and $\\frac{2i}{2}=i=x$. \u2713\n\n**If $x=-i$:** similarly all variables equal $-i$. \u2713\n\n**No mixed case:** solving $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}=i$ gives the unique solution $t=i$ (the quadratic $it^2+2t-i=0$ has discriminant $0$), so $i$ in any position forces *all* variables to be $i$ (likewise for $-i$).\n\n## Verifying the Domain Conditions\n\nFor $\\theta=\\frac{k\\pi}{15}$, the denominators $1-x^2,\\,1-y^2,\\,1-z^2,\\,1-w^2$ vanish only if some $\\tan(2^j\\theta)=\\pm1$, i.e., $2^j\\cdot\\frac{k\\pi}{15}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi$. This requires $4\\cdot 2^j\\,k=\\pm 15+60n$, which is **impossible** since the left side is even and the right side is odd. \u2713\n\n## Complete Solution Set\n\n$$\\boxed{\\left(\\tan\\tfrac{k\\pi}{15},\\;\\tan\\tfrac{2k\\pi}{15},\\;\\tan\\tfrac{4k\\pi}{15},\\;\\tan\\tfrac{8k\\pi}{15}\\right)\\quad\\text{for }k=0,1,\\dots,14, \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad (i,i,i,i),\\;(-i,-i,-i,-i)}$$\n\n**Total: 17 solutions.**\n\n## Structure (Orbits under Doubling)\n\nThe map $k\\mapsto 2k \\pmod{15}$ partitions $\\mathbb{Z}/15\\mathbb{Z}$ into:\n\n| Orbit | Size | Representative solution |\n|:---:|:---:|:---|\n| $\\{0\\}$ | 1 (fixed) | $(0,\\,0,\\,0,\\,0)$ |\n| $\\{1,2,4,8\\}$ | 4 | $\\left(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15},\\,\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15},\\,\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15},\\,\\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}\\right)$ |\n| $\\{5,10\\}$ | 2 | $(\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\sqrt{3},\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| $\\{3,6,12,9\\}$ | 4 | $\\left(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5},\\,\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5},\\,-\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5},\\,-\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right)$ |\n| $\\{7,14,13,11\\}$ | 4 | $\\left(\\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15},\\,-\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15},\\,\\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15},\\,-\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}\\right)$ |\n\nThe two solutions $(\\pm i,\\pm i,\\pm i,\\pm i)$ are fixed points of the map $t\\mapsto\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ that lie outside the tangent parametrization.",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this system of equations. The key observation is that the expression $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ is the double-angle formula for tangent. Specifically, if $t = \\tan(\\theta)$, then $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2} = \\tan(2\\theta)$.\n\nSo the system becomes:\n- $y = \\tan(2\\theta_1)$ where $x = \\tan(\\theta_1)$\n- $z = \\tan(2\\theta_2)$ where $y = \\tan(\\theta_2)$\n- $w = \\tan(2\\theta_3)$ where $z = \\tan(\\theta_3)$\n- $x = \\tan(2\\theta_4)$ where $w = \\tan(\\theta_4)$\n\nThis means:\n- $y = \\tan(2 \\arctan x)$\n- $z = \\tan(4 \\arctan x)$\n- $w = \\tan(8 \\arctan x)$\n- $x = \\tan(16 \\arctan x)$\n\nWait, let me be more careful. Let $\\theta = \\arctan x$, so $x = \\tan\\theta$.\n\nThen $y = \\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1-\\tan^2\\theta} = \\tan(2\\theta)$.\nThen $z = \\frac{2\\tan(2\\theta)}{1-\\tan^2(2\\theta)} = \\tan(4\\theta)$.\nThen $w = \\frac{2\\tan(4\\theta)}{1-\\tan^2(4\\theta)} = \\tan(8\\theta)$.\nThen $x = \\frac{2\\tan(8\\theta)}{1-\\tan^2(8\\theta)} = \\tan(16\\theta)$.\n\nSo we need $x = \\tan(16\\theta)$ where $x = \\tan\\theta$.\n\nThis gives us $\\tan(16\\theta) = \\tan(\\theta)$, which means $16\\theta = \\theta + k\\pi$ for some integer $k$, i.e., $15\\theta = k\\pi$.\n\nSo $\\theta = \\frac{k\\pi}{15}$ for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThen $x = \\tan\\left(\\frac{k\\pi}{15}\\right)$.\n\nBut we need to be careful about the domain. The equations are defined when $x^2 \\neq 1$, $y^2 \\neq 1$, $z^2 \\neq 1$, $w^2 \\neq 1$.\n\nLet me check: $x = \\tan(k\\pi/15)$. We need $x^2 \\neq 1$, i.e., $\\tan^2(k\\pi/15) \\neq 1$, i.e., $k\\pi/15 \\neq \\pi/4 + n\\pi$ for any integer $n$. Since $k$ ranges over integers, $k\\pi/15 = \\pi/4 + n\\pi$ would require $k/15 = 1/4 + n$, so $k = 15/4 + 15n$. This is never an integer, so this condition is always satisfied.\n\nWait, but I also need to check that $y^2 \\neq 1$, $z^2 \\neq 1$, $w^2 \\neq 1$.\n\n$y = \\tan(2k\\pi/15)$. We need $2k\\pi/15 \\neq \\pi/4 + n\\pi$, i.e., $2k/15 \\neq 1/4 + n$, i.e., $2k \\neq 15/4 + 15n$. Again, $15/4$ is not an integer, so this is always satisfied.\n\n$z = \\tan(4k\\pi/15)$. We need $4k\\pi/15 \\neq \\pi/4 + n\\pi$, i.e., $4k/15 \\neq 1/4 + n$, i.e., $4k \\neq 15/4 + 15n$. Again, $15/4$ is not an integer, so this is always satisfied.\n\n$w = \\tan(8k\\pi/15)$. We need $8k\\pi/15 \\neq \\pi/4 + n\\pi$, i.e., $8k/15 \\neq 1/4 + n$, i.e., $8k \\neq 15/4 + 15n$. Again, $15/4$ is not an integer, so this is always satisfied.\n\nSo all values $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ work, but we need to account for periodicity. $\\tan$ has period $\\pi$, so $\\tan(k\\pi/15) = \\tan(k'\\pi/15)$ when $k \\equiv k' \\pmod{15}$.\n\nSo there are exactly 15 distinct values of $x$: $x_k = \\tan(k\\pi/15)$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 14$.\n\nFor each such $x_k$, we get:\n- $y_k = \\tan(2k\\pi/15)$\n- $z_k = \\tan(4k\\pi/15)$\n- $w_k = \\tan(8k\\pi/15)$\n\nAnd the quadruple is $(x_k, y_k, z_k, w_k) = \\left(\\tan\\frac{k\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{2k\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{4k\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8k\\pi}{15}\\right)$.\n\nWait, but I need to verify that this actually works. Let me check: if $x = \\tan(k\\pi/15)$, then $y = \\tan(2k\\pi/15)$. Then we need $z = \\frac{2y}{1-y^2} = \\tan(2 \\cdot 2k\\pi/15) = \\tan(4k\\pi/15)$. \u2713\n\nThen $w = \\frac{2z}{1-z^2} = \\tan(2 \\cdot 4k\\pi/15) = \\tan(8k\\pi/15)$. \u2713\n\nThen $\\frac{2w}{1-w^2} = \\tan(2 \\cdot 8k\\pi/15) = \\tan(16k\\pi/15)$.\n\nNow, $16k\\pi/15 = k\\pi + k\\pi/15$. So $\\tan(16k\\pi/15) = \\tan(k\\pi + k\\pi/15) = \\tan(k\\pi/15) = x$. \u2713\n\nSo all 15 values work.\n\nBut wait, I should also consider the case where some of the intermediate values are undefined. We already checked that none of the denominators vanish, so we're fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The problem asks for solutions in $\\mathbb{C}^4$. The tangent function approach works over the reals (or at least for real angles). But since we're working over $\\mathbb{C}$, I need to be more careful.\n\nActually, the identity $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2} = \\tan(2\\arctan t)$ is an algebraic identity that holds over $\\mathbb{C}$ as well (where defined). So the approach still works.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The map $f(t) = \\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ is a rational function. We're looking for points where $f^4(t) = t$ (a 4th iterate of $f$ equals the identity), with the additional constraint that $f(t) \\neq \\pm\\infty$ at each step (i.e., $t \\neq \\pm 1$ at each step).\n\nActually, let me think about this as a conjugacy. The map $t \\mapsto \\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ is conjugate to the doubling map on the circle. Specifically, if we let $t = \\tan\\theta$, then $f(t) = \\tan(2\\theta)$. So the map is conjugate to $\\theta \\mapsto 2\\theta$ on $\\mathbb{R}/\\pi\\mathbb{Z}$ (or on the Riemann sphere via $t = \\tan\\theta$).\n\nThe 4th iterate corresponds to $\\theta \\mapsto 16\\theta$. We need $16\\theta \\equiv \\theta \\pmod{\\pi}$, i.e., $15\\theta \\equiv 0 \\pmod{\\pi}$, i.e., $\\theta = k\\pi/15$ for $k = 0, 1, \\ldots, 14$.\n\nSo the 15 solutions are:\n$$\\left(\\tan\\frac{k\\pi}{15},\\; \\tan\\frac{2k\\pi}{15},\\; \\tan\\frac{4k\\pi}{15},\\; \\tan\\frac{8k\\pi}{15}\\right) \\quad \\text{for } k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 14.$$\n\nLet me list them out:\n\n- $k=0$: $(0, 0, 0, 0)$\n- $k=1$: $(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15})$\n- $k=2$: $(\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{15})$\n- $k=3$: $(\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{6\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{12\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{24\\pi}{15})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{5})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}{5})$\n\nWait, let me be more careful. $\\tan(12\\pi/15) = \\tan(4\\pi/5) = -\\tan(\\pi/5)$. And $\\tan(24\\pi/15) = \\tan(8\\pi/5) = \\tan(8\\pi/5 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(-2\\pi/5) = -\\tan(2\\pi/5)$.\n\nSo $k=3$: $(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5})$\n\n- $k=4$: $(\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{32\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(16\\pi/15) = \\tan(16\\pi/15 - \\pi) = \\tan(\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(32\\pi/15) = \\tan(32\\pi/15 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(2\\pi/15)$\n  So $k=4$: $(\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15})$\n\n- $k=5$: $(\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{3})$\n  $= (\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})$\n\nLet me verify: $x = \\sqrt{3}$, $y = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{-2} = -\\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n$z = \\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{-2} = \\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n$w = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3}$. \n\nWait, that gives $w = -\\sqrt{3}$, but I computed $\\tan(8\\pi/3) = \\tan(8\\pi/3 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(2\\pi/3) = -\\sqrt{3}$.\n\nLet me recompute. $\\tan(8\\pi/3) = \\tan(8\\pi/3 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(2\\pi/3) = -\\sqrt{3}$.\n\nSo $k=5$: $(\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3})$\n\nLet me verify: $x = \\sqrt{3}$, $y = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n$z = \\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n$w = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n$\\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\sqrt{3} = x$. \u2713\n\n- $k=6$: $(\\tan\\frac{6\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{12\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{24\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{48\\pi}{15})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{5})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5})$\n\n  $\\tan(24\\pi/15) = \\tan(8\\pi/5) = \\tan(8\\pi/5 - \\pi) = \\tan(3\\pi/5)$... wait.\n  $\\tan(8\\pi/5) = \\tan(8\\pi/5 - \\pi) = \\tan(3\\pi/5) = -\\tan(2\\pi/5)$.\n  $\\tan(48\\pi/15) = \\tan(16\\pi/5) = \\tan(16\\pi/5 - 3\\pi) = \\tan(\\pi/5)$.\n\n  So $k=6$: $(\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5})$\n\n- $k=7$: $(\\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{14\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{28\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{56\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(14\\pi/15) = \\tan(14\\pi/15 - \\pi) = \\tan(-\\pi/15) = -\\tan(\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(28\\pi/15) = \\tan(28\\pi/15 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(8\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(56\\pi/15) = \\tan(56\\pi/15 - 3\\pi) = \\tan(11\\pi/15) = \\tan(\\pi - 4\\pi/15) = -\\tan(4\\pi/15)$\n\n  So $k=7$: $(\\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15})$\n\n- $k=8$: $(\\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{32\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{64\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(16\\pi/15) = \\tan(\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(32\\pi/15) = \\tan(2\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(64\\pi/15) = \\tan(64\\pi/15 - 4\\pi) = \\tan(4\\pi/15)$\n\n  So $k=8$: $(\\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15})$\n\n- $k=9$: $(\\tan\\frac{9\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{18\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{36\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{72\\pi}{15})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{3\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{6\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{12\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{24\\pi}{5})$\n  $\\tan(3\\pi/5) = -\\tan(2\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(6\\pi/5) = \\tan(\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(12\\pi/5) = \\tan(2\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(24\\pi/5) = \\tan(24\\pi/5 - 4\\pi) = \\tan(4\\pi/5) = -\\tan(\\pi/5)$\n\n  So $k=9$: $(-\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5})$\n\n- $k=10$: $(\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{3}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{3})$\n  $= (-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})$\n\n  Wait: $\\tan(16\\pi/3) = \\tan(16\\pi/3 - 5\\pi) = \\tan(\\pi/3) = \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n  So $k=10$: $(-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})$\n\n  Let me verify: $x = -\\sqrt{3}$, $y = \\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n  $z = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3}$.\n\n  Hmm, that gives $z = -\\sqrt{3}$, not $\\sqrt{3}$. Let me recheck.\n\n  $\\tan(4\\pi/3) = \\tan(\\pi/3) = \\sqrt{3}$. So $y = \\sqrt{3}$.\n  $\\tan(8\\pi/3) = \\tan(8\\pi/3 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(2\\pi/3) = -\\sqrt{3}$. So $z = -\\sqrt{3}$.\n  $\\tan(16\\pi/3) = \\tan(16\\pi/3 - 5\\pi) = \\tan(\\pi/3) = \\sqrt{3}$. So $w = \\sqrt{3}$.\n\n  So $k=10$: $(-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})$\n\n  Let me verify: $x = -\\sqrt{3}$, $y = \\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n  $z = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n  $w = \\frac{2(-\\sqrt{3})}{1-3} = \\sqrt{3}$. \u2713\n  $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{1-3} = -\\sqrt{3} = x$. \u2713\n\n- $k=11$: $(\\tan\\frac{11\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{22\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{44\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{88\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(11\\pi/15) = \\tan(\\pi - 4\\pi/15) = -\\tan(4\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(22\\pi/15) = \\tan(22\\pi/15 - \\pi) = \\tan(7\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(44\\pi/15) = \\tan(44\\pi/15 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(14\\pi/15) = -\\tan(\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(88\\pi/15) = \\tan(88\\pi/15 - 5\\pi) = \\tan(13\\pi/15) = -\\tan(2\\pi/15)$\n\n  So $k=11$: $(-\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15})$\n\n- $k=12$: $(\\tan\\frac{12\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{24\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{48\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{96\\pi}{15})$\n  $= (\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{16\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{32\\pi}{5})$\n  $\\tan(4\\pi/5) = -\\tan(\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(8\\pi/5) = -\\tan(3\\pi/5) = \\tan(2\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(16\\pi/5) = \\tan(\\pi/5)$\n  $\\tan(32\\pi/5) = \\tan(32\\pi/5 - 6\\pi) = \\tan(2\\pi/5)$\n\n  So $k=12$: $(-\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{\\pi}{5}, \\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{5})$\n\n- $k=13$: $(\\tan\\frac{13\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{26\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{52\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{104\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(13\\pi/15) = -\\tan(2\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(26\\pi/15) = \\tan(26\\pi/15 - \\pi) = \\tan(11\\pi/15) = -\\tan(4\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(52\\pi/15) = \\tan(52\\pi/15 - 3\\pi) = \\tan(7\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(104\\pi/15) = \\tan(104\\pi/15 - 6\\pi) = \\tan(14\\pi/15) = -\\tan(\\pi/15)$\n\n  So $k=13$: $(-\\tan\\frac{2\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15})$\n\n- $k=14$: $(\\tan\\frac{14\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{28\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{56\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{112\\pi}{15})$\n  $\\tan(14\\pi/15) = -\\tan(\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(28\\pi/15) = \\tan(28\\pi/15 - 2\\pi) = \\tan(8\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(56\\pi/15) = \\tan(56\\pi/15 - 3\\pi) = \\tan(11\\pi/15) = -\\tan(4\\pi/15)$\n  $\\tan(112\\pi/15) = \\tan(112\\pi/15 - 7\\pi) = \\tan(7\\pi/15)$\n\n  So $k=14$: $(-\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{8\\pi}{15}, -\\tan\\frac{4\\pi}{15}, \\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{15})$\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me just present the answer in the general form.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check whether there might be solutions that the tangent substitution misses. The tangent substitution $t = \\tan\\theta$ covers all of $\\mathbb{C}$ (as a map from $\\mathbb{C}$ to the Riemann sphere, it's a bijection from $\\mathbb{C}$ to $\\hat{\\mathbb{C}} \\setminus \\{i, -i\\}$... no wait.\n\nActually, $\\tan: \\mathbb{C} \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is not surjective. The values that $\\tan$ never takes are $i$ and $-i$ (since $\\tan\\theta = i$ would require $e^{2i\\theta} = \\frac{i+i}{i-i} = \\frac{2i}{-2i} = -1$, so $e^{2i\\theta} = -1$, which means $2i\\theta = i\\pi(2n+1)$, so $\\theta = \\pi(2n+1)/2$, and at those points $\\tan$ is undefined).\n\nWait, let me reconsider. $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{1}{i}\\frac{e^{i\\theta} - e^{-i\\theta}}{e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta}} = \\frac{1}{i}\\frac{e^{2i\\theta} - 1}{e^{2i\\theta} + 1}$.\n\nSo $\\tan\\theta = w$ iff $\\frac{1}{i}\\frac{e^{2i\\theta} - 1}{e^{2i\\theta} + 1} = w$, which gives $e^{2i\\theta} - 1 = iw(e^{2i\\theta} + 1)$, so $e^{2i\\theta}(1 - iw) = 1 + iw$, so $e^{2i\\theta} = \\frac{1+iw}{1-iw}$.\n\nThis has a solution iff $\\frac{1+iw}{1-iw}$ is on the unit circle (if we're looking for real $\\theta$) or, over $\\mathbb{C}$, it always has