nk again.\n\nIf $t = \\tan\\alpha$ and $s = \\tan\\beta$, then $\\frac{2t}{1-t^2} = \\tan(2\\alpha)$, so $s = \\tan(2\\alpha) = \\tan\\beta$ iff $2\\alpha = \\beta + n\\pi$ for some integer $n$.\n\nSo from $y = \\frac{2x}{1-x^2}$, if $x = \\tan\\alpha$ and $y = \\tan\\beta$, then $2\\alpha = \\beta + n_1\\pi$.\n\nFrom $z = \\frac{2y}{1-y^2}$, if $y = \\tan\\beta$ and $z = \\tan\\gamma$, then $2\\beta = \\gamma + n_2\\pi$.\n\nFrom $w = \\frac{2z}{1-z^2}$, if $z = \\tan\\gamma$ and $w = \\tan\\delta$, then $2\\gamma = \\delta + n_3\\pi$.\n\nFrom $x = \\frac{2w}{1-w^2}$, if $w = \\tan\\delta$ and $x = \\tan\\alpha$, then $2\\delta = \\alpha + n_4\\pi$.\n\nAdding all: $2(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta) = (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta) + (n_1 + n_2 + n_3 + n_4)\\pi$.\n\nSo $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = (n_1 + n_2 + n_3 + n_4)\\pi$.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me take a different approach.\n\nActually, I think the cleaner way is to note that the map $f(t) = \\frac{2t}{1-t^2}$ satisfies $f(\\tan\\theta) = \\tan(2\\theta)$ whenever $t = \\tan\\theta$ is defined and $t \\neq \\pm 1$ (to avoid division by zero).\n\nSo if $x = \\tan\\theta$ (with $x \\neq \\pm 1$), then $y = f(x) = \\tan(2\\theta)$ (assuming $y \\neq \\pm 1$), and so on.\n\nThe condition $y \\neq \\pm 1$ is equivalent to $\\tan(2\\theta) \\neq \\pm 1$, i.e., $2\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/4 + n\\pi$, i.e., $\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/8 + n\\pi/2$.\n\nSimilarly, we need $\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/16 + n\\pi/4$ (for $z$ to be defined), and $\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/32 + n\\pi/8$ (for $w$ to be defined), and $\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/64 + n\\pi/16$ (for the last equation to be defined).\n\nBut wait, we also need $x \\neq \\pm 1$, i.e., $\\theta \\neq \\pm\\pi/4 + n\\pi$.\n\nHmm, but actually, the condition is just that the denominators are nonzero. Let me re-examine.\n\nThe system requires:\n1. $1 - x^2 \\neq 0$, i.e., $x \\neq \\pm 1$.\n2. $1 - y^2 \\neq 0$, i.e., $y \\neq \\pm 1$.\n3. $1 - z